Consortium Server — Detected Bugs and Test Defects
===================================================
Logged during E2E test refactoring (2026-06-25)
================================================

## TEST DEFECTS (bugs in the original test code, now fixed)

[TEST-BUG-01] conftest.py — delete_agent_generators_after_test used wrong URL
  File: tests/api_tests/conftest.py (original)
  Problem: The cleanup fixture called admin_session.delete(
      f"http://localhost:9999/api/listeners/{agent_generator_id}")
    instead of /api/agent-generators/{agent_generator_id}.
    Agent generators were never actually deleted, allowing state to leak
    between tests.
  Fix: Changed URL to /api/agent-generators/{agent_generator_id} in new conftest.py.

[TEST-BUG-02] test_listeners_api.py — test_update_listener used PUT instead of PATCH
  File: tests/api_tests/test_listeners_api.py (original), line 254
  Problem: session.put(f"http://localhost:9999/api/listeners/{listener_id}", ...)
    The listeners API only exposes PATCH for updates; PUT is not registered.
    This would always return 405 Method Not Allowed, never testing the actual
    update path.
  Fix: Changed to client.patch(...) in the refactored test.

[TEST-BUG-03] test_listeners_api.py — LISTENER_JSON_SCHEMA had wrong required key
  File: tests/api_tests/test_listeners_api.py (original)
  Problem: The inline status sub-schema defined "state" in properties but listed
    "status" in the required array:
      "required": ["status", "error"]  # should be ["state", "error"]
    The StatusModel (component_models.py) serialises as {"state": ..., "error": ...}.
    JSON Schema validation would always fail for the status sub-object because
    the required key "status" was absent from actual responses.
  Fix: Changed required to ["state", "error"] in LISTENER_JSON_SCHEMA.

[TEST-BUG-04] framework_components_json_response_schemas.py — STATUS_JSON_SCHEMA used wrong key
  File: tests/api_tests/framework_components_json_response_schemas.py
  Problem: STATUS_JSON_SCHEMA defined the status field as {"status": {"type": "string"}}
    with "required": ["status", "error"], but StatusModel serialises the field
    as "state", not "status". Any test validating agent generator or agent generator
    build-step status objects against this schema would always fail.
  Fix: Changed property key and required entry from "status" to "state".

[TEST-BUG-05] test_user_accounts_api.py — wrong error code in schema
  File: tests/api_tests/test_user_accounts_api.py (original)
  Problem: DUPLICATE_USER_ACCOUNT_CREATION_ERROR_JSON_SCHEMA specified error code
    "DUPLICATE_USER_ACCOUNT_CREATION_ERROR". The server actually raises
    UserAccountUsernameAlreadyExistsError which serialises as
    "USER_ACCOUNT_USERNAME_ALREADY_EXISTS_ERROR".
    The schema was defined but never used in any active test, so the mismatch
    went undetected.
  Fix: Renamed to USER_ACCOUNT_USERNAME_ALREADY_EXISTS_ERROR_JSON_SCHEMA with the
    correct error code and added a test that exercises the duplicate-username path.

[TEST-BUG-06] conftest.py — _JSON_WEB_TOKEN_JSON_SCHEMA used wrong field name
  File: tests/api_tests/conftest.py (original)
  Problem: The schema required "type" but JSONWebTokenModel (user_models.py) and
    JSONWebToken.to_json() both serialise the field as "token_type". Any schema
    validation on the login response would have failed.
  Fix: Updated to require "token_type".

[TEST-BUG-07] test_logout_api.py — operator_session accidentally excluded from RBAC check
  File: tests/api_tests/test_logout_api.py (original), line 190
  Problem: The RBAC denial loop was written as:
      for session in [spectator_session]:  # , spectator_session]:
    The comment indicates operator_session was meant to be included but was
    accidentally left out. Operator does not have LOGOUT_USER_ACCOUNT_BY_USER_ACCOUNT_ID
    permission, so this left the operator RBAC path untested.
  Fix: Refactored test_logout_user_account_by_user_account_id to iterate over both
    operator_client and spectator_client for the 403 assertions.


## SERVER-SIDE ISSUES (do not fix — report only)

[SERVER-BUG-01] user_accounts_api.py — duplicate username returns 422 instead of 409
  File: consortium/server/api/user_accounts_api.py, create_user_account endpoint
  Problem: When a POST /api/user-accounts request supplies a username that already
    exists, the server raises UserAccountUsernameAlreadyExistsError and returns
    HTTP 422 Unprocessable Entity. Per RFC 9110 / REST conventions, creating a
    duplicate resource is a conflict and should return 409 Conflict.
    The same pattern applies to PATCH /api/user-accounts/{id} and
    PATCH /api/user-accounts/me when a duplicate username is specified.
  Severity: Low (semantic correctness / API design)

[SERVER-BUG-02] agents_api.py — GET /api/agents/tasks requires undeclared permission READ_ALL_AGENT_TASKS
  File: consortium/server/api/agents_api.py, get_all_agent_tasks endpoint
  Problem: The endpoint uses AuthorizeUserRequest(UserPermissions.READ_ALL_AGENT_TASKS)
    but server_dependencies.py ROLE_PERMISSIONS maps spectators and operators to
    READ_ALL_AGENT_TASKS_BY_AGENT_ID rather than READ_ALL_AGENT_TASKS.
    If READ_ALL_AGENT_TASKS is not in any role's permission set the endpoint would
    always return 403 for all roles.
  Severity: Medium (potential complete denial of service for the endpoint)

[SERVER-BUG-03] user_accounts_api.py — update_own_user_account and update_user_account_by_user_account_id are synchronous (def not async def)
  File: consortium/server/api/user_accounts_api.py, lines 226 and 298
  Problem: These route handlers are declared with def rather than async def.
    While FastAPI can handle synchronous handlers (running them in a thread pool),
    the inconsistency with all other handlers in the file (which are async) may
    cause unexpected blocking behaviour under load.
  Severity: Low (functional but potentially blocking)

[SERVER-BUG-04] listeners_api.py — PATCH /api/listeners/{listener_id} requires UPDATE_LISTENER_BY_LISTENER_ID but the original tests used PUT
  File: consortium/server/api/listeners_api.py
  Problem: No PUT /api/listeners/{listener_id} endpoint is defined; only PATCH.
    The original test_listeners_api.py (line 254) used session.put(...) which
    would have returned 405 Method Not Allowed, never exercising the update path.
    This means the PATCH update path was never tested in production runs.
  Severity: Low (test coverage gap, server code itself is correct)
